findings.body:
The PR removes optional chaining from `openForm` in `FormWrapper` (`!!openForm.in_reply_to_snippet`). `MainForm` renders `FormWrapper` without the `openForm` prop, and the prop is optional. So whenever `isOpen` is true, that is when the main editor is focused or has content, the render throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. That unmounts the comments widget for every visitor who starts typing a top-level comment.

The same wrapper still uses `openForm?.in_reply_to_id` a few lines later, which shows the `?.` was intentional. The e2e suite types into the main form in `test/e2e/editor.test.ts`, so these tests should fail too.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="311-315"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Restore the optional chaining. The header already relies on it at line 382.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="313-313"}
        const isReplyToReply = !!openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet;

The payload changed from `openForm.in_reply_to_id` to `parent.id`. For a reply to a reply, `ReplyFormBox` is rendered inside the top-level `PublishedComment` with `comment` set to the top-level comment (comment.tsx:152), so `parent` is the top-level comment. The user's actual target, the reply they clicked, is in `openForm.in_reply_to_id`. After this change it is dropped.

The server then runs `comments-service.js` (around line 366). Because the referenced comment, the top-level one, has `parent_id` null while `parent` is the top-level id, `inReplyToComment` is set to null. The reply is saved with no `in_reply_to_id`. The "replying to" attribution is lost, and nothing fails loudly. The change also isn't needed for the stated bug, because the header fallback is what needed removing.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="24-39"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Revert this line. Keep the `openForm` dependency in the `useCallback` deps array.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/reply-form.tsx" lines="30-30"}
                in_reply_to_id: openForm.in_reply_to_id,

The header fix only needed the fallbacks removed. This also changes `setEditable` from `!!memberName` to `!!member?.expertise`, so a logged-in member with a name but no expertise can no longer type. The comment directly above still says "Disable editing if the member doesn't have a name", so the code and comment disagree.

`focusEditor` (form.tsx around line 356) still gates on `memberName`. A member with a name but no expertise clicks the editor, gets a non-editable editor, and never gets the prompt. That is a dead end. It is a product decision that belongs in its own change with a description of the new rule.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="258-265"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Either restore `!!memberName` (the original gate) or make the rule consistent everywhere and update the comment. If expertise-gating is intended, split it into its own PR.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="264-265"}
        // Disable editing if the member doesn't have a name or when we are submitting the form
        editor.setEditable(!!memberName && progress !== 'sending');
    }, [editor, memberName, progress]);

The new test `Reply form does not inherit expertise from parent comment author` sets the logged-in member to `expertise: null`. With the gating change in `form.tsx`, `setEditable(false)` makes the editor non-focusable. The test then calls `waitEditorFocused(editor)`, which asserts that the inner `editor` test id is focused. That will time out.

I did not run the suite, so this is based on reading the code, but the dependency is direct. If the editability change is reverted, the test may still pass. Either way it should assert only on the header, not on focus, for a member who cannot type.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/test/e2e/actions.test.ts" lines="630-649"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the gating stays, drop `waitEditorFocused` for the no-expertise case and assert that the editor is not editable instead.

The test `Reply form does not inherit name from parent comment author` ends with `expect(memberName).not.toHaveText('Named Author')`. That passes whenever the locator resolves to nothing or to a different string, so it cannot tell whether the header shows the right thing. `FormHeader` renders `name ? name : 'Anonymous'` under `member-name`, so the test should assert that placeholder directly.

::code{file="apps/comments-ui/test/e2e/actions.test.ts" lines="676-679"}

::callout{type="info"}
Asserting `toHaveText('Anonymous')` would catch both the original bug and any regression that shows a blank header.
